<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
        <RootNamespace>Acme.Dms.Identity</RootNamespace>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
    Declared as properties so CI can point this at the two versions it just packed without
    editing a tracked file. Neither default is produced by anything, which is the point: a
    developer who builds this without packing first gets a restore failure naming a version
    that does not exist, rather than a quiet success against whatever was lying around.

      ./build-dms.ps1 Package -PackageTarget Identity -Configuration Release
      ./build-dms.ps1 Package -PackageTarget Plugins -Configuration Release

      Import-Module ./package-helpers.psm1
      ./eng/verification/Invoke-IdentityConsumerCheck.ps1 `
        -PackageVersion (Get-IdentityContractVersion) `
        -PluginsPackageVersion (Get-PluginsContractVersion) `
        -NuGetPackagesDirectory (Join-Path ([System.IO.Path]::GetTempPath()) "nuget-verify-$([guid]::NewGuid().ToString('N'))")

    Each contract's version is its own surface version and not the DMS release version; the
    two readers above read the files that declare them. They are passed separately because
    they move independently of each other.

    Both are pinned exactly, in [brackets]. A bare version is a floor, not a pin, so with more
    than one nupkg in the local folder feed NuGet resolves a different one and warns via NU1603
    instead of failing, which would let this check validate an artifact other than the one just
    packed.
  -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <IdentityPackageVersion Condition="'$(IdentityPackageVersion)' == ''"
            >0.0.0-local</IdentityPackageVersion
        >
        <PluginsPackageVersion Condition="'$(PluginsPackageVersion)' == ''"
            >0.0.0-local</PluginsPackageVersion
        >
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
    Deliberately outside central package management, and carrying no ProjectReference into
    src/ - a project reference would defeat the entire point of the check. It is in no .sln and
    nothing in the repository builds it except the runner beside it.

    It restores both contracts because the samples the identity guide publishes are a provider
    and the plugin that registers it, and the plugin half needs EdFi.Api.Plugins.

    The two Microsoft.Extensions packages are declared here rather than leaned on transitively,
    because the plugin sample names IConfiguration and IServiceCollection and an implementer
    writing it compiles against both assemblies directly. Their versions are deliberately
    independent of src/Directory.Packages.props and of Dependabot, but they cannot sit below the
    floor EdFi.Api.Plugins declares: a lower version here is a downgrade that fails this restore.
  -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="EdFi.Api.Identity" Version="[$(IdentityPackageVersion)]" />
        <PackageReference Include="EdFi.Api.Plugins" Version="[$(PluginsPackageVersion)]" />
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" Version="10.0.1" />
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.1" />
    </ItemGroup>
</Project>
